WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:19.145
 

00:00:19.145 --> 00:00:20.520
PRESENTER 1: This
block of stores

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:24.430
was located at 84 Massachusetts
Avenue, across the street

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:28.590
from MIT's main entrance.

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:31.590
One of MIT's many connections
to public television,

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:35.610
this is where Boston station
WGBH first went on the air

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:38.970
in 1955.

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:41.800
The building had previously been
the home of a roller skating

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:46.610
rink, and the wooden floor,
bumpy from years of moisture,

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:49.970
posed a challenge for
cameras dollying across it

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:52.980
and made creaking noises.

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:57.300
Nonetheless, in the six years
at the MIT campus location,

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:02.020
WGBH alumni say, this is where
many successful careers were

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:04.170
launched and important
broadcast work

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:08.670
developed, including simulcasts
on radio and TV of Boston

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:12.580
Symphony Orchestra concerts,
with some early ones taped

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:16.610
at MIT's Kresge Auditorium.

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:20.050
After a fire in 1961
destroyed the studios,

00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:22.185
WGBH moved to other locations.

00:01:22.185 --> 00:01:24.980
 

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:27.200
MIT President, James Killian, was

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:30.190
a leader in the development of WGBH

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:32.640
and played other important roles in the early days

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.920
of educational television, including

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.540
as chairman of the National Commission on

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:42.450
Educational Television and later, director

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:44.850
of the Corporation for Public Broadcasting.

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:47.830
FITCH: Hello, I'm John
Fitch, MIT science reporter--

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:49.950
PRESENTER 1: In the
1960s, John Fitch

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:52.090
hosted the Science
Reporter television series

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:55.536
broadcast, produced
jointly by MIT and WGBH.

00:01:55.536 --> 00:01:58.440
 

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:01.890
A pioneering effort at having
scientists explain their work

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:04.060
to a general
audience, the series

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:07.200
was another MIT/WGBH
connection, which

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:11.030
had been encouraged
by President Killian.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:13.017
FITCH: Dr. Harold
Edgerton, a professor

00:02:13.017 --> 00:02:14.850
of electrical measurements
in the Department

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:17.040
of Electrical
Engineering at MIT.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.600
A man who was both an
inventor and a teacher

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:23.130
and internationally famous as
the father of modern high speed

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:24.090
photography.

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:27.640
FITCH: The way it worked was
that WGBH called me and asked

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:31.620
me if I'd be willing to-- would I like to try out for this MIT

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:34.480
Science Reporter program.

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:37.200
And I thought, yeah, that sounds really interesting.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:41.530
Over the next four years, we did just about

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:43.720
100 half hour programs.

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:46.260
PRESENTER 1: MIT's
relationship with WGBH

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:50.490
came into play again when
in 2012 Kresge Auditorium

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:54.820
was host of an MIT produced
performance documentary.

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:57.330
PRESENTER 2: Next, an
orchestra of students from one

00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:00.640
of the world's great science
and engineering universities.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:02.990
PRESENTER 1: A film featuring
the premiere performance

00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:05.500
of Awakening by the
MIT Wind Ensemble.

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:08.480
 

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:12.100
WGBH Boston aired the
program the following year,

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:13.630
and it went on to
win an Emmy award.

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:17.090
 

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:19.410
So in this part
of the MIT campus,

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:22.800
where WGBH had its start, to
where the Stratton Student

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:26.280
Center is now, we
come full circle

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:29.586
to remember where a piece of
broadcasting history was made.

00:03:29.586 --> 00:03:33.824
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:33.824 --> 00:03:48.460
 

